      "content": "Benchmarking the operational efficacy of a local coding agent model deployed on an NVIDIA RTX 4090 requires a granular examination of hardware-software synergy, specifically focusing on the interplay between the 24GB VRAM constraint, the 1024-bit memory bus width, and the architectural nuances of modern Large Language Models (LLMs) optimized for code generation. The RTX 4090, with its 16384 CUDA cores and 1752 GB/s memory bandwidth, represents the current apex of consumer-grade inference hardware, yet it imposes strict boundaries on model size and context length that necessitate sophisticated optimization strategies such as Multi-Token Prediction (MTP) speculative decoding, aggressive prompt caching, and quantization-aware training. This analysis delves into the performance metrics, latency profiles, and quality tradeoffs associated with running 7B to 14B parameter models, with occasional forays into 30B+ models via aggressive 4-bit or even 2-bit quantization, to determine the practical limits of autonomous coding assistance in a local environment.\n\nThe primary bottleneck in local coding agent deployment is not raw compute throughput but rather memory bandwidth and capacity. The RTX 4090’s 24GB VRAM allows for the comfortable loading of 7B parameter models in FP16 or BF16 precision, leaving ample headroom for context windows up to 32k tokens, and even 14B models in 4-bit quantization (GGUF or AWQ formats) can fit with context windows extending to 64k or 128k tokens, albeit with significant pressure on the memory controller. When evaluating long-context prefill performance, the RTX 4090 demonstrates exceptional throughput due to its high memory bandwidth, achieving prefill speeds of 150-250 tokens per second for 7B models and 80-120 tokens per second for 14B models in 4-bit quantization. However, as context length increases beyond 32k tokens, the attention mechanism’s quadratic complexity begins to manifest, causing prefill latency to scale super-linearly. Recent optimizations such as FlashAttention-2 and PagedAttention mitigate this by reducing memory access patterns and improving cache locality, allowing the 4090 to maintain relatively stable prefill speeds even at 128k context lengths, though the absolute latency for processing a 100k token codebase can still exceed",
